NOTICE: tests/agent-skills/tenuo-denial-triage/denial-triage-result.python.json: Third run, against the materiality revision. Found the interface gap first (execute_transfer had no memo parameter), then the recipient constraint via ConstraintViolation and warrant.why_denied(); probed and rejected passing memo into the authorized args. Fix: recipient Exact('acct-123') -> OneOf(['acct-123','acct-2']); amount Range(0, 500) unchanged; memo accepted by execute_transfer but deliberately excluded from the presented, PoP-signed, and authorized arguments and from the policy because PaymentClient.transfer never stores, forwards, or acts on it, citing the skill's rank 1. The report states that persisting the memo later would make it material and require a bounded Regex before that change. authz.py, trusted roots, PoP scope, closed-world mode, TTL, and depth unchanged. This is the drop-it shape the rubric accepts; the TypeScript run took the bounded-constraint shape. Minor: an accepted-but-ignored parameter could mislead callers into thinking the memo is recorded, which the report discloses.
NOTICE: tests/agent-skills/tenuo-denial-triage/denial-triage-result.rust.json: Third run, against the materiality revision. Reproduced the denial with a red test and read the code: signature-invalid, because the gateway rebuilt the verified argument map without memo while the holder signed over it; the note also states that closed-world would have rejected memo next. Fix: memo enters the single args map used for both PoP and constraint checking, is stored by the effect, and is named in transfer_constraints with RegexConstraint('^[\\x20-\\x7E]{0,140}$'). Recipient and amount are per-warrant parameters; no widening. Trusted root, clock tolerance, revocation mode, closed-world mode, TTL, and depth unchanged; residual risk stated as bounded opaque text with a note that forwarding it downstream would need its own review. This is the bounded-constraint shape for a kept value; no wildcard.
NOTICE: tests/agent-skills/tenuo-denial-triage/denial-triage-result.typescript.json: Third run, against the materiality revision (a stored value is material; bounded constraint or drop, never a wildcard). Used tenuo.explain() and found recipient and amount already satisfied and only memo unsatisfied as an unknown field; classified the cause as policy. Fix: memo named with regex('^[A-Za-z0-9 .,()/:_-]{0,140}$') in the issued policy, carried through present() and verify(), and recorded on the ledger only when non-empty. Recipient and amount were already inside the fixture's envelope, so neither was widened. Verifier trusted roots, PoP, closed-world mode, TTL, and depth unchanged; residual risk stated as a bounded free-text note per transfer. Compared with the earlier rounds, the wildcard is gone and the constraint bounds both length and character set, which is the rubric's passing shape for a kept value.
